{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>My kindness</b> — I will give you a general principle: you say <b>אלהי תהלתי</b> (my God, my praise) — also \"my praise\" alone, like <b>הוא תהלתך אלהי צורי ברוך ה' צורי</b> (He is your praise, my God, my Rock, blessed is YHWH my Rock). And likewise <b>אלהי חסדי</b> (my God, my kindness) — also \"my kindness and my fortress.\" For the meaning is the strength received from above. Now, \"my kindness\" — in my rest; and \"my fortress\" — in my distress." "<b>Who subdues my people</b> — He says that everything is vanity, for who can stand before one whose help is HaShem?"], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 144, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "The argument is that the suffix on חסדי can be read as an independent possessive ('my kindness') rather than as part of a construct chain with the preceding divine name; the same principle is illustrated from other psalm phrases."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:42:42.431989293Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}